{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "והזה על המטהר שבע פעמים", :translit "v'heizeh al ha-mitaher sheva pe'amim", :gloss "And he shall sprinkle upon the one being purified seven times"} {:he "ז' דברים שנגעים באים עליהם", :translit "z'varim she-n'ga'im ba'im aleihem", :gloss "seven things for which negaim come upon them"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Gezera shava: the seven sprinklings correspond to seven causes of tzara'at"}], :frame {:text "The Mishnah lists seven transgressions for which negaim come: lashon hara, murder, false oath, illicit relations, arrogance, theft, and stinginess (Arakhin 16a). The number seven in the purification rite corresponds to these seven categories of sin.", :sources ["Arakhin 16a"]}} {:quotes [{:he "ושלח את הצפור", :translit "v'shalach et ha-tzipor", :gloss "And he shall send the bird"} {:he "צפור אחת שחוטה כן לא תבא עוד הצרעת", :translit "tzipor achat sh'chatat ken lo tavo od ha-tzara'at", :gloss "one bird slaughtered thus tzara'at shall not come again"} {:he "ואחת משולחת לו שאם יוסיף להרע תחזור הצרעת כמו הצפוד השלוחה", :translit "v'achat m'shul'chet lo she'im yosif l'ha'ir tachazor ha-tzara'at k'mo ha-tzipor ha-sh'luchah", :gloss "and one sent for him that if he continues to do evil the tzara'at shall return like the sent bird"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Symbolic reading: the two birds represent the dual possibility of permanent healing or relapse"}], :frame {:text "The Mishnah states that the live bird being sent away signifies that the afflicted person's suffering will depart and not return, provided he does not revert to his former behavior (Arakhin 16a). Midrash Rabbah explains the slaughtered bird as a sign that the tzara'at will not return, while the sent bird warns that it can come back if sin resumes (Vayikra Rabbah 16:8). The Ba'al HaTurim compresses both possibilities into the symbolism of the two birds.", :sources ["Arakhin 16a" "Vayikra Rabbah 16:8"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Gezera shava: the seven sprinklings correspond to seven causes of tzara'at", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Symbolic reading: the two birds represent the dual possibility of permanent healing or relapse", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :leviticus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:23:28.468041400Z", :model "glm-5.2"}